  "query_preview": "..\n\nPartly sunny conditions as a result of mixed mid and high clouds \ncharacterizes weather across SE Mi today. The clouds are tied to mid \nlevels of the shallow frontal zone stalled in the Ohio valley this \nmorning. The entire frontal zone shifts slightly northward this \nafternoon, directing the mid level thermal/moisture gradients \nfarther into northern Lower Mi. Temperatures are primarily \ninfluenced by surface high pressure centered on the Ontario/Quebec \nborder supplying a steady east boundary layer wind. Cooler air is \nreinforced initially this morning, however advection weakens \nconsiderably this afternoon, which allows slightly above normal high \ntemperatures in the lower 60s south of I-69. Mid and upper 50s are \nmore common toward the Tri Cities and northern Thumb where thicker \nclouds last longer and the cool air mass is deeper. \n\nThe Ohio valley front begins a stronger northward move tonight as \nbroad low pressure develops in the lee of the Rockies and central \nCanada. Now a surface warm front, it reaches the Ohio border near \nthe west end of Lake Erie late tonight extending into SW Lower Mi. \nHREF and REFS mean surface Td rises to around 50 F toward sunrise \nwith an assist from Lake Erie and St Clair on light SE boundary \nlayer wind. The presence of the warm front and marine moisture \naugmentation presents higher probability of fog from the Ohio border \nup through the northern/western Detroit suburbs late tonight and \nWednesday morning. \n\nThe warm front moves from the Ohio valley today, into southern Lower \nMi late tonight, and then into the northern Great Lakes Wednesday \nafternoon while still not generating any rain over the area. Model \nmean RH and theta-e projections show the shallowness of the front \nmaintaining more productive isentropic lift over the upper Midwest \nand northern Great Lakes. New in the 27/00Z models is a southern \nstream short wave moving up from the Rio Grande valley that could \nproduce a stray shower on its own as the warm front exits Wednesday \nmorning. Otherwise, the weather highlight is warm sector high \ntemperatures rising back up to around 70 for the entire region. \nHighs in the mid 70s are also still in reach by Thursday afternoon, \nnear records for the date, as the next cold front approaches from \nthe Midwest.  \n\nLimiting factors for temperatures Thursday is clouds and a chance of \nshowers within the moisture axis that builds in ahead of the cold \nfront Thursday afternoon. The front itself is on schedule for \npassage across SE Mi Thursday evening followed by weak high pressure \nFriday. NBM guidance high temperatures maintain a minimal \ntemperature drop post front with highs in the 60s Friday through the \nweekend. The trend to monitor is the strength of an additional \nsouthern stream short wave originating from the split flow long wave \ntrough during Friday. Extended range models are in reasonable \nagreement on the track but vary in strength on a system moving up \nfrom the Rio Grande valley while drawing Gulf moisture northward for \na chance of showers in the Great Lakes by Sunday.",
